Developer helpers. Nothing here is built into the twin, linked against it,
or needed to compile, test, or run the project.

  twin_sim      headless CSV sim-runner. C source, built by the top-level
                CMake build into build/twin_sim(.exe).
  plot_run.py   plots a twin_sim CSV. Python 3, needs matplotlib
                (pip install -r tools/requirements.txt).


twin_sim

Steps the digital-twin core (engine + thermal + sync, plus the sensor
model) forward over a scripted throttle/altitude mission and writes a CSV
time-series to stdout. Run parameters and errors go to stderr, so a plain
redirect keeps the CSV clean. Links engine_core only; no SDL.

Build:

  cmake --build build --target twin_sim

Usage:

  twin_sim [--profile NAME] [--dt SECONDS] [--duration SECONDS]
           [--load NM] [--seed N] [--sensor] [--list] [--help]

  --profile NAME   mission profile (default: idle). -p also works.
  --dt SECONDS     integration step and CSV row spacing (default: 0.02)
  --duration SEC   total simulated time (default: the profile's own)
  --load NM        constant external load torque, N*m (default: 40)
  --seed N         RNG seed for the --sensor noise stream (default: 1)
  --sensor         add 10 noisy sensor columns next to ground truth
  --list           print the profile list and exit
  --help           print usage and exit

Profiles:

  idle             closed throttle, sea level
  takeoff          throttle ramp to WOT, ground roll then 10 m/s climb
  cruise-climb     0.75 throttle, 5 m/s climb capped at 4500 m
  hot-day          0.80 throttle, sea level, ISA +25 C
  rapid-throttle   0.20 <-> 1.00 square wave, 20 s period, sea level

Columns:

  t          simulated time, s
  throttle   commanded lever position, 0..1
  alt_m      geopotential altitude, m
  ambient_c  ISA outside-air temp at alt_m plus the profile offset, deg C
  rpm        crank speed
  map_kpa    manifold absolute pressure, kPa
  torque_nm  indicated torque, N*m
  cht_c      cylinder head temperature, deg C
  egt_c      exhaust gas temperature, deg C
  oil_c      oil temperature, deg C

With --sensor, each of rpm, map, cht, egt, oil also gets a noisy value
column and an _ok flag (1 valid, 0 dropped; value is 0 when dropped):

  s_rpm s_rpm_ok s_map_kpa s_map_ok s_cht_c s_cht_ok
  s_egt_c s_egt_ok s_oil_c s_oil_ok

The sensor stream is deterministic for a given --seed.

Examples:

  twin_sim --list
  twin_sim --profile cruise-climb > runs/cc.csv
  twin_sim -p rapid-throttle --sensor --dt 0.01 --duration 90 > runs/rt.csv


plot_run.py

Draws a twin_sim CSV as a plain stacked multi-panel figure (default
matplotlib styling) with a two-line metadata header: speed/command,
temperature, manifold/torque, and altitude panels. Max markers are placed
on CHT and EGT. Columns are matched by name, so extra columns added later
are ignored rather than shifting the layout. --y overrides the layout with
an explicit column list on a single panel.

Run metadata (profile, load, seed) comes from twin_sim's '# ...' stderr
banner -- present in --sim mode; for a plain file, redirect with 2>&1 or it
shows as '--'. dt / duration / sample rate are always inferred from the data.

Setup:

  pip install -r tools/requirements.txt

Usage:

  python tools/plot_run.py CSV [options]
  python tools/plot_run.py -   [options]          read CSV from stdin
  python tools/plot_run.py [options] --sim TWIN_SIM_ARGS...

  --y COL [COL ...]   plot exactly these columns, skip the panel layout
  --sensor           overlay the s_* channels on their truth lines
  --limits           overlay caution / redline lines (off by default)
  --title TEXT       replace the metadata header with this text
  --out FILE         save the figure instead of opening a window
  --width  N         figure width in inches (default: 11)
  --height N         figure height in inches (default: 8)
  --twin-sim PATH    twin_sim binary for --sim (default: build/twin_sim)

--sim consumes every argument after it and hands them to twin_sim, so put
plot_run options before --sim. A --sensor inside the --sim args turns on
the overlay here too.

The --limits caution/redline values are placeholders in LIMITS at the top
of plot_run.py -- tune them alongside the physics constants.

Examples:

  python tools/plot_run.py runs/cc.csv
  python tools/plot_run.py runs/cc.csv --y cht_c egt_c oil_c
  python tools/plot_run.py --out runs/rt.png --sim -p hot-day --sensor
  twin_sim -p hot-day | python tools/plot_run.py -


Notes

Run outputs belong in runs/ (gitignored). CSVs are regenerable from
twin_sim, so don't commit them.

plot_run.py reads the CSV as UTF-8 with an optional BOM and skips '#'
lines, so a PowerShell-redirected file (UTF-16 BOM) or one with the
stderr banner merged in still loads.
